       " {'MedlineCitation': DictElement({'KeywordList': [ListElement([StringElement('Cognition', attributes={'MajorTopicYN': 'N'}), StringElement('Neural cell adhesion molecule', attributes={'MajorTopicYN': 'N'}), StringElement('Schizophrenia', attributes={'MajorTopicYN': 'N'}), StringElement('Synaptic plasticity', attributes={'MajorTopicYN': 'N'})], attributes={'Owner': 'NOTNLM'})], 'CitationSubset': [], 'GeneralNote': [], 'OtherID': [], 'InvestigatorList': [], 'OtherAbstract': [], 'SpaceFlightMission': [], 'PMID': StringElement('42712863', attributes={'Version': '1'}), 'DateCompleted': {'Year': '2026', 'Month': '09', 'Day': '09'}, 'DateRevised': {'Year': '2026', 'Month': '09', 'Day': '10'}, 'Article': DictElement({'ArticleDate': [DictElement({'Year': '2024', 'Month': '12', 'Day': '13'}, attributes={'DateType': 'Electronic'})], 'Language': ['eng'], 'ELocationID': [StringElement('10.1007/s12291-024-01290-0', attributes={'EIdType': 'doi', 'ValidYN': 'Y'})], 'Journal': {'ISSN': StringElement('0970-1915', attributes={'IssnType': 'Print'}), 'JournalIssue': DictElement({'Volume': '41', 'Issue': '5', 'PubDate': {'Year': '2026', 'Month': 'Oct'}}, attributes={'CitedMedium': 'Print'}), 'Title': 'Indian journal of clinical biochemistry : IJCB', 'ISOAbbreviation': 'Indian J Clin Biochem'}, 'ArticleTitle': 'Neural Cell Adhesion Molecule-1 Gene Polymorphism Increases the Risk of Cognitive Dysfunction in Schizophrenia Spectrum Disorder.', 'Pagination': {'StartPage': '792', 'EndPage': '798', 'MedlinePgn': '792-798'}, 'Abstract': {'AbstractText': [StringElement('Neural cell adhesion molecule-1 (NCAM-1), a marker of synaptic plasticity is reported to be altered and associated with cognitive dysfunction in schizophrenia. The aim of the study was to analyse the allele and genotype frequency of neural cell adhesion molecule-1 (NCAM-I) gene polymorphism (rs1836796, rs2303377, rs584427, rs646558) and plasma NCAM-I levels in schizophrenia and their association with cognitive function. Two hundred and sixteen (216) schizophrenia patients and 216 controls were enrolled in the study. NCAM-I polymorphism and its plasma levels were analysed in cases and controls. Cognitive status was evaluated using ACE-III scores. The rs 1836796 genotype was associated with cognitive status (p\\u2009=\\u20090.011) in schizophrenia. Among the genotypes of rs 1836796, the TT variant (OR: 2.193, 95% CI: 1.097-4.386, p\\u2009=\\u20090.0292) conferred a potential increased risk of schizophrenia. Attention score (p\\u2009<\\u20090.05), memory score (p\\u2009<\\u20090.01), fluency score (p\\u2009<\\u20090.01), language score (p\\u2009<\\u20090.01), Visuospatial abilities score (p\\u2009<\\u20090.01) and total ACE III scores (p\\u2009<\\u20090.01) were significantly reduced; negative symptom score (p\\u2009<\\u20090.01), general psychopathological Score (p\\u2009<\\u20090.01) and total PANSS score (p\\u2009<\\u20090.01) was significantly increased in TT genotype compared to GG genotype. We conclude that single nucleotide polymorphisms of NCAM-I increase the risk of schizophrenia and are related to severity of the disease and cognitive impairment.', attributes={'Label': 'UNLABELLED'}), StringElement('The online version contains supplementary material available at https://doi.org/10.1007/s12291-024-01290-0.', attributes={'Label': 'SUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION', 'NlmCategory': 'UNASSIGNED'})], 'CopyrightInformation': '© The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Association of Clinical Biochemists of India 2024. Springer Nature or its licensor (e.g. a society or other partner) holds exclusive rights to this article under a publishing agreement with the author(s) or other rightsholder(s); author self-archiving of the accepted manuscript version of this article is solely governed by the terms of such publishing agreement and applicable law.'}, 'AuthorList': ListElement([DictElement({'Identifier': [], 'AffiliationInfo': [{'Identifier': [StringElement('https://ror.org/02fq2px14', attribu