       " {'MedlineCitation': DictElement({'KeywordList': [ListElement([StringElement('Cardiovascular health', attributes={'MajorTopicYN': 'N'}), StringElement('High-sensitivity C-reactive protein', attributes={'MajorTopicYN': 'N'}), StringElement('Inflammation', attributes={'MajorTopicYN': 'N'}), StringElement('Lead', attributes={'MajorTopicYN': 'N'}), StringElement('Occupational health', attributes={'MajorTopicYN': 'N'}), StringElement('Selenium', attributes={'MajorTopicYN': 'N'})], attributes={'Owner': 'NOTNLM'})], 'CitationSubset': [], 'GeneralNote': [], 'OtherID': [], 'InvestigatorList': [], 'OtherAbstract': [], 'SpaceFlightMission': [], 'PMID': StringElement('42500346', attributes={'Version': '1'}), 'DateCompleted': {'Year': '2026', 'Month': '07', 'Day': '25'}, 'DateRevised': {'Year': '2026', 'Month': '07', 'Day': '26'}, 'Article': DictElement({'ArticleDate': [DictElement({'Year': '2024', 'Month': '11', 'Day': '18'}, attributes={'DateType': 'Electronic'})], 'Language': ['eng'], 'ELocationID': [StringElement('10.1007/s12291-024-01271-3', attributes={'EIdType': 'doi', 'ValidYN': 'Y'})], 'Journal': {'ISSN': StringElement('0970-1915', attributes={'IssnType': 'Print'}), 'JournalIssue': DictElement({'Volume': '41', 'Issue': '4', 'PubDate': {'Year': '2026', 'Month': 'Aug'}}, attributes={'CitedMedium': 'Print'}), 'Title': 'Indian journal of clinical biochemistry : IJCB', 'ISOAbbreviation': 'Indian J Clin Biochem'}, 'ArticleTitle': 'Occupational Exposure to Lead Increases Inflammation that Causes Cardiovascular Disease and Lowers Selenium levels.', 'Pagination': {'StartPage': '626', 'EndPage': '632', 'MedlinePgn': '626-632'}, 'Abstract': {'AbstractText': [StringElement(\"Lead is a hazardous heavy metal. It has serious health effects due to its unique physical and chemical properties. Lead poisoning can induce inflammatory signaling pathways and oxidative stress. This course decreases resistance to diseases and infections. This study investigates the risks of occupational exposure to Lead in petroleum industry workers and its effect on the increase of high-sensitivity reactive protein (hs-CRP), which indicates an increased risk of heart disease. The study also investigated the antagonistic relationship between Lead and Selenium, as the latter is an important component of the antioxidant defense system. The study included two groups of males: the first group included ninety individuals working in oil well sites in Basrah, and the second group included ninety individuals who were not exposed. The results indicate high levels of toxic Lead, <i>P</i>\\u2009≥\\u20090.0001, and a significant increase in high-sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs-CRP), <i>P</i>\\u2009≥\\u20090.0001, in the worker's serum compared to the control group. In addition, there was a decrease in Selenium levels (<i>P</i>\\u2009≥\\u20090.001) in the worker's blood compared with the control group. High-sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs-CRP) was measured using a device (Finecare Plus, FS-112/FS-113/FS-205, Wondfo, China). Lead and Selenium levels were determined using an ICP-OES device (HORIBA Scientific, JY2000, France).\", attributes={'Label': 'UNLABELLED'}), StringElement('Explaining the mechanism of lead toxicity.', attributes={'Label': 'GRAPHICAL ABSTRACT', 'NlmCategory': 'UNASSIGNED'}), StringElement('The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1007/s12291-024-01271-3.', attributes={'Label': 'SUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION', 'NlmCategory': 'UNASSIGNED'})], 'CopyrightInformation': '© The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Association of Clinical Biochemists of India 2024. Springer Nature or its licensor (e.g. a society or other partner) holds exclusive rights to this article under a publishing agreement with the author(s) or other rightsholder(s); author self-archiving of the accepted manuscript version of this article is solely governed by the terms of such publishing agreement and applicable law.'}, 'AuthorList': ListElement([DictElement({'